I'm Russell Brauer and I'm director of  audio and the lead composer of Blizzard  Entertainment as audio director of  Blizzard my job is to first and foremost  help create an environment where this  wonderful staff that I have the great  honor of working with feel safe to be  creative and do everything it takes to  affect the sound of everything we do so  every sound that Blizzard makes whether  it's in the game whether it's on the web  with a cinematic if it makes sound we  have something to do with so my job is  to kind of keep the Train on the rails  and occasionally lead by example in my  case I do write music as well the advice  I would give to someone who's just  starting out relates to that finding  your passion statement I was lucky  enough to find out at a relatively young  age what I really love to do and in this  case for me it was music and sound and  in finding that I found the thing that I  would I would go home every night if I  had a day job or whatever I would go  home every night to my studio and create  music and sound just because I had to it  was burning a hole in my belly and I and  so I tell people you need to want it  that much that you would do it any way  that you would do it if you had no job  you'd still do it if you had to pay  because it's a student film maybe that  you're scoring or a student video game  like you would have these days you would  just do it for free or even pay to do it  you might have to pay the musicians out  of your own pocket if you as a person  getting started out can can hear me say  all that and said yes that's me I can do  that  I'll stay glued to it then you probably  have a chance if you get to do what you  love it's there's just nothing better I  mean there's no better way to enjoy  your your passion and to contribute and  to leave something in this world better  than you found it leave some art behind  leave some you beautiful music whatever  it is that's the best thing you can do  and so if you if you've got the  perseverance for it please go for it at  Blizzard we're known for three major  franchises the Warcraft franchise  specifically now World of Warcraft the  Diablo franchise and Starcraft and they  are very different from each other and  even though it's the same group of  people of us we number about 40 strong  now in the sound Department it's a very  big responsibility to maintain the  individual identity of each of these  franchises we even record the different  franchises with different orchestras in  different size rooms in very specific  places Warcraft has a kind of Bronze Age  sound so we record that in a in an old  church chapel if you will with a six  60-foot ceiling and it just has this  sound that's very specific and with  Diablo we've relied heavily on choral  music and we had a specific choir for  the demonic side and another one for the  heavenly side Diablos are really unique  franchise for us in that the music is  very iconic for a lot of our fans and  players Diablo was the first thing we  ever played in fact I was the first game  I ever played  long before I ever knew I would work at  Blizzard the soundtrack that Matt Ullman  created for Diablo 1 Diablo 2 and the  expansion for Diablo 2 is it's unusual  it's something that you wouldn't expect  it's 12 string guitar through a delay  and it has these iconic chords and it it  doesn't make intuitive sense but it  completely works and that became  ingrained and in the hearts and memories  of everyone who loved the game Matt had  started experimenting with more live  orchestras and this vog marryin sound so  we picked up that thread  and ran with it and went with this very  large orchestra sound in fact we  recorded the orchestra in a the most  old-fashioned concert hall we could like  the oldest way you can hear music short  of being in an amphitheater is in this  big hall and then we added this choral  element again with this Irish choir a  nuna which is a very gorgeous sound for  the quote-unquote hell choir and then  playing against type this all-male just  nasty sounding choir for the heaven side  and that sort of support the story ideas  that maybe the evil sides a little more  seductive and beautiful and heaven has  in fighting going on there's turmoil  going on and so much so that Diablo  could walk in through the side door and  start kicking the tar out of everybody  so in the end we're storytellers  everyone at Blizzard is a storyteller no  matter what their discipline and the  things I just mentioned are some of the  devices we used as sound and music folks  to to help tell that story I think sound  music especially is a game of contrasts  so the epic moments are more epic if you  have either quiet moments or moments of  silence our goal is for people to feel  immersed and hopefully keep the sound  turned off I've been fortunate enough my  career to work in just about every  medium there is television a little film  not a lot but a little toys and a lot of  theme parks as well as games and there's  a common thread storytelling a  storytelling and a linear medium like  film or television is really important  because you do learn a lot about what I  mentioned earlier about contrasts or  setting up a moment and then paying it  off and learning something about pacing  and how to work with timing to picture  and things like that these are all  skills that are really great to have  because in a video game  well usually something can happen at the  most unexpected time you still have  these opportunities to tell a mini story  through sound with a series of like  sonic events that aren't just randomly  put together in a hodgepodge but  literally have some deliberate pacing to  them so designing sound for a theme park  is really a lot like designing sound for  world of warcraft I think of all the  warcraft is a theme park in a box but  I'd have to say technology is a  double-edged sword because the fact that  we could now play a hundred two hundred  three hundred a thousand sounds at once  isn't necessarily a good thing one of  the reasons people to this day still  love the music from the old oldest video  games Mario or what-have-you is because  the technology was so limited they had  to make the melody count with only one  note playing at a time or maybe two or  four so the melodies had to be good and  be memorable and so again using music as  an example the best music would sound  just as powerful on a music box as it  would with a symphony orchestra so a lot  of what we're doing these days is making  sure that we don't just use the  technology for its own sake I think one  of the big frontiers ahead is simulating  what mixers do when they dub a movie  they have every voice every sound effect  every piece of music on a different  volume control or fader and  scene-by-scene they decide what's  important and what moves the the story  forward and turn off the rest so I think  the big it's both a boon and a challenge  that we can play all the sounds we want  it's all very clean but now the  challenge is to use great responsibility  to not to not overdo it
 

